remaining tiles\\n- The hand structure is close to: 2 exposed chows + [W3, W4, W5] chow + need 1 more meld + pair\\n\\nLooking at unshown tiles:\\n- W2 (3 left), W5 (3 left), T2 (4 left), T3 (3 left)\\n\\nBest waiting structure analysis:\\n- Discarding **T6**: Leaves [W3, W4, W5, W5, T1, T2, T2] - can wait for W2 to form [W2, W3, W4] or complete with T1-T2-T3 structure\\n- Discarding **W3**: Breaks the W3-W4-W5 potential chow\\n- Discarding **T1**: Leaves [W3, W4, W5, W5, T2, T2, T6] - harder to complete\\n\\nThe optimal play is to discard **T6** because:\\n1. It's isolated and doesn't contribute to any meld formation\\n2. Keeping W3-W4-W5-W5 maintains a complete chow + pair\\n3. Keeping T1-T2-T2 allows flexibility to form either a pung or chow with T3\\n4. This maximizes my waiting tiles\\n\\nReason: T6 is an isolated tile that doesn't fit into any productive meld structure. By discarding it, I maintain the strongest hand structure with [W3, W4, W5] forming a potential chow, [W5] as a pair, and [T1, T2, T2] which can develop into either a pung (if I draw T2) or contribute to a chow sequence (with T3). This gives me the most flexible waiting pattern and best chance to complete a winning hand.\\n\\nAnswer: Play T6\"}"}
